HISTORY
\f0\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\
\

\fs28 ##Ancient Origins (2000 BCE \'96 300 BCE)
\f2\b\fs24 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 Where: Egypt, Greece\
\
Characteristics: Early forms of pleating appeared in Egyptian linen kilts and in Greek peplos and chitons, which used hand-set pleats.\
\
Fabric: Fine linen (Egypt), lightweight wool and linen (Greece).\
\
Technique: Pleats were created manually and maintained through starching or repeated pressing.\
\
\

\fs28 ##19th Century Revival (1870s\'961890s)\

\fs24 \
\
Technological shift: The Industrial Revolution allowed machine-set pleats.\
\
Fashion context: Victorian skirts and petticoats sometimes used pleated panels for volume and decoration.\
\
Fabric: Silk taffeta, cotton, wool blends.\
\
Style: Mostly decorative pleating placed at hems or on underskirts.\
\
\

\fs28 ##Early 20th Century Modernism (1909\'961920s)
\fs24 \
\
\
Key figure: Mariano Fortuny patented his Delphos gown (1909), using heat-set micropleating inspired by ancient Greece.\
\
Fabric: Luxurious silk treated to hold very fine pleats permanently.\
\
Shift: Move toward fluidity, freedom of movement, and long vertical lines.\
\
\

\fs28 ##Mid-Century Popularity (1950s)\

\fs24 \
\
Fashion context: Christian Dior\'92s New Look (1947 onward) inspired full, feminine silhouettes.\
\
Fabric: Stiff or medium-weight fabrics (cotton, wool crepe, synthetic blends) that created\
structured A-line pleats.\
\
Style: Defined waists, knee-to-mid-calf lengths, and crisp accordion pleats.\
\
\

\fs28 ##1960s\'961980s Variation and Mass Production
\fs24 \
\
\
1960s: Mini pleated skirts (often polyester blends) became youthful and sporty.\
\
1970s: Softer pleats in chiffon or rayon for boho and disco styles.\
\
1980s: Knife pleats in synthetics dominated school uniforms and office wear.\
\
\

\fs28 ##2000s\'96Today
\fs24 \
\
\
Technological improvements: Thermoplastic fibers allow long-lasting pleats that resist washing.\
\
Fabric: Polyesters, chiffons, satins, and metallic fabrics.\
\
Style: Midi skirts with knife or sunburst pleats; mix of streetwear and high fashion; emphasis on movement and shine.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs72 \cf0 #TYPES OF PLEATS\

\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Knife Pleats
\fs24 \
\
Narrow folds pressed in the same direction, typically 1\'962 cm wide, allowing controlled movement. Used by Yves Saint Laurent and Raf Simons to create clean, elongated lines in skirts and suiting, relying on heat-setting for crisp permanence.\
\

\fs28 ##Box Pleats
\fs24 \
\
Two knife pleats folded away from each other to form a structured \'93box,\'94 adding volume without bulk at the waist. A hallmark of Christian Dior\'92s New Look, where they were engineered to hold shape using stiffened underlayers or organza interfacings.\
\

\fs28 ##Accordion/Pliss\'e9 Pleats
\fs24 \
\
Very fine, evenly spaced pleats that expand and contract with motion. First achieved by Mariano Fortuny through a secret heat-and-\
roller process on silk; later reimagined by Issey Miyake, who used thermoplastic fibers and industrial heat-setting for permanent flexibility.\
\

\fs28 ##Sunburst (Fan) Pleats
\fs24 \
\
Pleats that radiate from a single point, narrower at the waist and wider at the hem, creating dramatic movement. Madeleine Vionnet used sunburst pleating on bias-cut gowns, manipulating grainlines to enhance fluidity and flare.\
\

\fs28 ##Cartridge Pleats\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
Deep, rounded pleats created by gathering fabric with evenly spaced stitches rather than pressing, enabling large volumes to attach to small waistbands. Revived by John Galliano in couture, often supported with tulle or crinoline to maintain sculptural form.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Draped/Couture Pleats
\fs24 \
\
Soft, multidirectional pleating formed by hand rather than machine, often pinned and shaped directly on the mannequin. Madame Gr\'e8s mastered this technique using fine silk jersey, coaxing fabric into sculptural folds with meticulous hand-working and steam-setting.
\fs25 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
\
\

\fs72 #ICONIC PLEATED SKIRTS\

\fs24 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Marilyn Monroe Circa 1955\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
The legendary Marilyn Monroe's drafty subway-grate moment is likely the first (and most famous) wardrobe malfunction ever caught on camera. While it's easy to get distracted by her mischievous smile and toned calves, we should also take a moment to admire the ivory pleated halter dress designed by Hollywood costumer William Travilla.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Madame Gr\'e9s Circa 1945\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
This gown exemplifies the signature style of Madame Gr\'e8s, the legendary French couturi\'e8re known for her mastery of sculptural drapery and Grecian-inspired design. The dress features her hallmark finely hand-pleated jersey, wrapped and twisted around the bodice with architectural precision. Its fluid, floor-lengthsilhouette and soft, cascading folds embody her philosophy of \'93sculpting with fabric,\'94 creating an ethereal, timeless elegance that feels both restrained and deeply luxurious\'97an unmistakable tribute to Gr\'e8s\'92 unparalleled craftsmanship.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Issey Miyake Circa 2013\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
Japanese designer Issey Miyake is pretty much the master of pleating, known for his experimental expertise in the realm. He actually created a technique known as garment pleating, which is a way of creating the folds with memory, and he started the dedicated line Pleats Please in 1993. While he retired from his namesake line in 1997, the house still stays true to the original cutting-edge aesthetic. For fall 2013, creative director Dai Fujiwara sent this eye-catchingly printed and delicately pleated dress down the runway.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Christian Dior Spring 2014\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
Raf Simons updated the folds for his garden-party-inspired Christian Dior spring 2014 collection with draped bias pleats paired with an architectural cutout halter top. A sexed-up classic.\
\
\

\fs72 #MARKETS
\fs24 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 ##High-End Fashion\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Concept, Craftsmanship, and Innovation\
\
In the luxury segment, skirts often serve as vehicles for craftsmanship, sculptural form, and technological experimentation.\
\
High-end brands prioritize:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Innovative materials (heat-set pleats, bonded textiles, organza, treated leather)\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Architectural tailoring (draping, asymmetric constructions, experimental volumes)\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Artistic storytelling and runway context\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Limited production and hand-finishing\
\pard\tx566\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 For example, Issey Miyake\'92s pleated skirts rely on proprietary pleating processes, while brands like Comme des Gar\'e7ons or Maison Margiela often treat skirts as conceptual objects\'97challenging the wearer\'92s expectations of proportion, gender, and function. The result is a garment that prioritizes expression over efficiency, often existing at the intersection of clothing and art.\
\
**Example with description**\
\
this is a straight high waisted midi skirt, close to the body but not tight with an elastic waistband\
the hem remains soft and minimal\'97no lining, no closure\'97because the engineered pleats keep everything in place.\
polyester with heat-set permanent micro-knife pleats\
the vertical pleats create a slimming, column-like silhouette and elongate the body visually.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

