\fs28 \cf0 ##Mass Production\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \
Efficiency, Versatility, and Commercial Demand\
In contrast, mass-market skirts are shaped by:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Cost-effective materials (polyester blends, cotton, viscose)\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
High-capacity pattern cutting and automated pleating\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Stable, easily marketable silhouettes\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Trend responsiveness rather than trend creation\
\pard\tx566\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Fast-fashion and mid-market brands tend to favor styles that can be produced consistently at scale: A-line minis, everyday midis, elastic-waist pleated skirts, denim skirts, or simple flared silhouettes. Pleated skirts in particular became a mass-market staple because synthetic fabrics now hold pleats permanently, making them inexpensive to produce and easy to care for.\
\
**Example with description**\
\
polyester, midi-length skirt with high-waist elastic waistband\
\
the pleats are uniform, vertical knife-pleats (or similar), giving the skirt a clean, consistent look.\
